1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,553
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:08,970
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:08,970 --> 00:00:15,720
QUALCOMM, ticker QCOM triggered, then faded to -0.1 percent below the entry.

4
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:20,872
If it closes a week below the stop, the exit is automatic.

5
00:00:20,872 --> 00:00:24,424
That is not a failure; that is the plan.

6
00:00:24,424 --> 00:00:29,360
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

7
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:34,769
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

8
00:00:34,734 --> 00:00:38,393
On the day, the book is down 637 dollars.

9
00:00:38,393 --> 00:00:41,249
Avnet (AVT) leads, +5.9 percent.

10
00:00:41,249 --> 00:00:43,838
BGC Group (BGC) lags at -2.8.

11
00:00:43,838 --> 00:00:48,657
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

12
00:00:48,657 --> 00:00:52,184
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

13
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:59,689
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

14
00:00:59,689 --> 00:01:09,247
11 positions, led by AbbVie, ticker ABBV, Arista Networks, ticker ANET, Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing, ticker TSM.

15
00:01:09,247 --> 00:01:20,169
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

16
00:01:20,143 --> 00:01:25,071
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

17
00:01:25,071 --> 00:01:27,535
Every morning OROS proposes.

18
00:01:27,535 --> 00:01:38,358
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

19
00:01:38,358 --> 00:01:42,406
Inside the book today: 3 positions up, 8 down.

20
00:01:42,406 --> 00:01:44,782
Best, AVT at +5.94 percent.

21
00:01:44,782 --> 00:01:46,542
Worst, BGC at -2.81.

22
00:01:46,542 --> 00:01:51,646
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

23
00:01:51,646 --> 00:01:58,950
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.98% per trade over 984 graded positions.

24
00:01:58,950 --> 00:02:06,165
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

25
00:02:06,165 --> 00:02:11,445
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

26
00:02:11,445 --> 00:02:24,117
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 196 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

27
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:26,392
Over to the sports desk.

28
00:02:26,392 --> 00:02:35,887
Day 58 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

29
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:40,875
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 102,454.

30
00:02:40,875 --> 00:02:47,972
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 2.3 thousand back at 100,201.

31
00:02:47,972 --> 00:02:55,357
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 4.9 thousand back at 97,522.

32
00:02:55,357 --> 00:02:58,043
The race has no finish line.

33
00:02:58,043 --> 00:03:02,455
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

34
00:03:02,455 --> 00:03:07,442
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

35
00:03:07,442 --> 00:03:09,360
Nothing is restated.

36
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:18,268
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

37
00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:25,565
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

38
00:03:25,565 --> 00:03:27,806
Nothing here is financial advice.

39
00:03:27,806 --> 00:03:30,795
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
